description:
crate-jmap-chat-types/src/message.rs has `SenderId` with three hand-rolled impls (`Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Display`). `Serialize` and `Display` carry the same logic in two places:

  impl Serialize for SenderId {
      fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
          s.serialize_str(match self {
              SenderId::Owner => "self",
              SenderId::Contact(id) => id.as_str(),
          })
      }
  }

  impl std::fmt::Display for SenderId {
      fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
          f.write_str(match self {
              SenderId::Owner => "self",
              SenderId::Contact(id) => id.as_str(),
          })
      }
  }

Idiomatic Rust: when `Serialize` collapses the type to a string, delegate to `Display` via `serializer.collect_str(self)`:

  impl Serialize for SenderId {
      fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
          s.collect_str(self)
      }
  }

One body, single source of truth. A future variant addition (e.g. a system-bot sentinel) only needs to update `Display` to land everywhere — including `format!`, log lines, error messages, and the JSON wire form. Today, adding a variant means remembering to touch two functions in two places, and the test suite has no cross-impl consistency check to catch a mismatch.

Maintenance risk is real but low (the impls are visually adjacent, mistakes would likely be noticed). Priority 3 reflects "cleanup, not bug". Filed under `traits` rather than `clippy` because the issue is API design, not a lint-level local rewrite.
